{
  "reviewedHead": "db7cc6a08ad714e37df49eb6ca7973db7bd4d468",
  "summary": "UX-040 implements provenance tracking across edge, sidecar, and client news pipeline. Architecture is sound: acquisition timestamps are independent of publisher dates; delivery-path distinctions (upstream / server_cache / memory_cache / persistent_cache) are preserved; malformed/empty/error are distinct at all boundaries; GDELT seendate is correctly mapped to observedAt (not publishedAt); legacy array shapes are preserved by legacyNewsItems(); tracked-v1 responses use no-store; fallback uses app-owned snapshot rows with a strict 2-hour age boundary; disabled/unconfigured clear retained rows; scope generation guards late async completions. Mutation evidence covers 31 cases with applied-diff, assertion failure, and restoration. Gate reports 85+21+15752+266+664 invocations, zero failures. Three nonblocking gaps found: (1) JS newsTimestamp in api/_news-provenance.js and sidecar lacks the `value <= now` guard present in TypeScript normalizeNewsTimestamp, so future-dated publications are classified 'known' on the edge/sidecar; this is self-healed when the client calls normalizeNewsProvenance (which rejects publishedAt > now \u2192 'unknown'), but the inconsistency between implementations is a maintenance risk. (2) cacheStoredAt in the list-feed-digest inner fetcher callback is stamped before cachedFetchJsonWithMeta writes to Redis, slightly preceding the actual store time. (3) In MediastackNewsPanel.load(), the early-return path for a superseded request does not reset this.loading to false; subsequent snapshot-driven renders may briefly display a loading indicator until the next load cycle completes.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "nonblocking",
      "path": "api/_news-provenance.js",
      "line": 18,
      "message": "newsTimestamp (line 16\u201319) returns Number.isFinite(parsed) ? parsed : null without the `parsed <= now` guard present in TypeScript's normalizeNewsTimestamp. Consequently, normalizeNewsRows (lines 37\u201340) classifies a successfully-parsed future date as publicationState 'known' rather than 'unknown'. This is self-corrected on the client when normalizeNewsProvenance calls normalizeNewsTimestamp (which rejects value > now), but the divergence between the JS and TypeScript canonical implementations is a latent maintenance hazard and could surface if the edge/sidecar output is consumed without the TypeScript normalization boundary."
    },
    {
      "severity": "nonblocking",
      "path": "server/crystalball/news/v1/list-feed-digest.ts",
      "line": 69,
      "message": "cacheStoredAt: Date.now() is captured inside the cachedFetchJsonWithMeta fetcher callback and embedded in the acquisition object before the function returns its result for storage. The actual Redis write occurs after the callback returns, so the recorded cacheStoredAt precedes the true cache-write time. The discrepancy is sub-millisecond in practice, but technically violates the field's stated semantics ('Cache write time, never an acquisition surrogate'). The inconsistency with rss.ts (which stamps cacheStoredAt immediately before feedCache.set) may also surprise future readers."
    },
    {
      "severity": "nonblocking",
      "path": "src/components/MediastackNewsPanel.ts",
      "line": 74,
      "message": "In load(), the early-return path taken when a request is superseded (this.disposed || sequence !== this.requestSequence) exits without setting this.loading = false. Any snapshot-subscription render that fires between the abandoned return and the start of the next load may see this.loading === true with no articles (renderFeed returns 'Loading news\u2026' when loading && articles.length === 0). Practical impact is limited \u2014 loading resolves on the next completed load \u2014 but a subscription notification arriving at just the right time after a chip-change race will display a stale loading indicator until the replacement request finishes."
    }
  ]
